
	PORTING SUBC TO DIFFERENT PLATFORMS

	As of version 2025-12-22, SubC uses a vtable-based architecture
	for code generation, which makes porting to new platforms much
	easier. All code generators are compiled into a single binary,
	and target selection happens at runtime via the -T flag.


	(1) Create a new architecture directory

	Create a new directory for your architecture under
	src/targets/arch/:

		mkdir src/targets/arch/NEWCPU

	Copy one of the existing code generator files as a template:

		cp src/targets/arch/i386/cg_i386.c \
		   src/targets/arch/NEWCPU/cg_NEWCPU.c


	(2) Implement the code generator vtable

	Edit the new file and implement all the functions in the
	cg_vtable structure. The vtable contains function pointers
	for all code generation operations:

	- Section control: cgdata, cgtext, cgprelude, cgpostlude, cgpublic
	- Load/store operations: cgldgb, cgldgw, cgstorib, cgstoriw, etc.
	- Arithmetic: cgadd, cgsub, cgmul, cgdiv, cgmod, etc.
	- Comparisons: cgeq, cgne, cglt, cggt, etc.
	- Branches: cgbrtrue, cgbrfalse, cgjump, etc.
	- Function calls: cgentry, cgexit, cgcall, cgcalr, etc.

	Also define the cg_arch structure with your architecture's
	properties:

		struct cg_arch cg_arch_NEWCPU = {
		    "NEWCPU",        /* name */
		    32,              /* bits */
		    1,               /* char_size */
		    2,               /* short_size */
		    4,               /* int_size */
		    4,               /* long_size */
		    4,               /* ptr_size */
		    4,               /* float_size */
		    8,               /* double_size */
		    4,               /* bpw (bytes per word) */
		    ENDIAN_LITTLE,   /* endian */
		    STACK_DOWN,      /* stack_dir */
		    ASM_GAS,         /* asm_syntax */
		    CC_CDECL,        /* call_conv */
		    ...
		    /* Compiler limits - 0 means use defaults */
		    0,               /* namelen (default: 32, HLASM: 8) */
		    0,               /* max_case (default: 1024) */
		    0,               /* max_break (default: 64) */
		    0,               /* max_locinit (default: 128) */
		    0,               /* max_fnargs (default: 127) */
		    0,               /* nsymbols (default: 1024) */
		    0,               /* poolsize (default: 16384) */
		    0,               /* nodepoolsz (default: 4096) */
		    0                /* label_prefix (default: 'L') */
		};

	Note: Compiler limits can be customized per architecture.
	For memory-constrained systems (8086), use smaller values.
	For architectures with naming restrictions (HLASM: 8 chars),
	set namelen accordingly.

	Note: Type sizes are architecture-specific. For example:
	  - 8086: int=2, long=4
	  - i386: int=4, long=4
	  - x86-64: int=8, long=8 (SubC uses 64-bit int on x86-64)


	(3) Add OS configuration (if needed)

	If your target OS is not already supported, add a new
	cg_os_config structure to src/targets/os/cg_os_configs.c:

		struct cg_os_config cg_os_NEWOS = {
		    OS_NEWOS,
		    OBJ_ELF,
		    "NewOS",
		    0,               /* symbol_underscore */
		    "as -o %s %s",   /* asm_cmd */
		    "ld -o %s %s/lib/%scrt0.o",  /* ld_cmd */
		    "",              /* sys_libc */
		    "a.out"          /* aout_name */
		};


	(4) Create target entries

	At the end of your cg_NEWCPU.c file, create cg_target entries
	for each OS/CPU combination:

		struct cg_target cg_target_NEWOS_NEWCPU = {
		    "newos-newcpu",
		    "NewOS NEWCPU (ELF, GAS syntax)",
		    &cg_arch_NEWCPU,
		    &cg_os_NEWOS,
		    &cg_vtable_NEWCPU,
		    NULL,
		    NULL
		};


	(5) Register the target

	Add your target to the registry in src/cgtarget.c:

		extern struct cg_target cg_target_NEWOS_NEWCPU;

		static struct cg_target *targets[] = {
		    ...
		    &cg_target_NEWOS_NEWCPU,
		    NULL
		};


	(6) Update the Makefile

	Add your new source file to the CG_ARCH variable in
	src/Makefile:

		CG_ARCH=targets/arch/i386/cg_i386.c \
		        targets/arch/x86_64/cg_x86_64.c \
		        ...
		        targets/arch/NEWCPU/cg_NEWCPU.c


	(7) Create an OS-specific C startup file

	Copy a crt0-*.s file from runtime/targets/ to create a new
	startup file:

		cp runtime/targets/freebsd-386/crt0-freebsd-386.s \
		   runtime/targets/NEWOS-NEWCPU/crt0-NEWOS-NEWCPU.s


	(8) Compile, test, repeat

	Rebuild the compiler:

		cd src && make clean && make scc0

	Test your new target:

		./scc0 -T newos-newcpu -S test.c
		cat test.s

	A lot of things *will* go wrong. Do not get discouraged! This
	is normal. First make the compiler emit syntactically correct
	assembler code. Then make it compile the library. Then test
	some small programs.

	Pointer arithmetics can be particularly nasty, so there is an
	ad-hoc pointer arithmetics test suite included in the SubC
	archive. Just run 'make ptest' in src/. If it does not print
	anything, fine!


	(9) Optionally: send your patches to the author

	Thank you!

